    <DataNode elementId="AT1G06820-MONOMER" textLabel="carotene isomerase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9M9Y8" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Mutants affected in the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana CRTISO&lt;/i&gt; gene accumulate acyclic, poly-&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-carotene precursors: |FRAME:CPD-7496 prolycopene|, |FRAME:CPD-7524 7,9,9&apos;-&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-neurosporene| and a mix of &amp;zeta;-carotene isomers |CITS:[11884677]|. The accumulation of these compounds leads to a distinctive orange-yellow coloration of the cotyledons, in constrast with the yellow coloration of those of wild type. The accumulation of &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-carotenes rapidly disappears upon exposure to light. The protein responsible for this mutant phenotype was identified as a carotenoid isomerase. Its activity was confirmed by heterologous expression in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;: extracts of mutant, dark-grown &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; mutants were co-incubated with recombinant CRTISO-expressing &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; extracts; the mixture was shown to consistently contain an increase in |FRAME:CPD1F-114 &lt;i&gt;all-trans&lt;/i&gt;-lycopene|, |FRAME:NEUROSPORENE neurosporene| and |FRAME:CPD1F-98 &lt;i&gt;all-trans&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;zeta;-carotene| |CITS:[11884677]|.</Comment>
